  "selection": {
    "rationale": "The 1 MiB candidate is selected primarily from explicit live-buffer bounds, bounded-call latency, reuse granularity, and sustained throughput. Across the 100 MiB generated workload, its median throughput was within 0.8 percent of the fastest candidate for both operations, while its maximum new-chunk and authenticated-read calls were 68.5 and 74.4 percent shorter than 4 MiB. Explicit maximum live chunk-buffer accounting was 1 MiB for new chunks and 2 MiB for authenticated reads, compared with 4 MiB and 8 MiB for the 4 MiB candidate. Same-worker absolute RSS high-water evidence corroborates the lower-memory direction but is treated as noisy process-level evidence rather than a precise allocator measurement. The extra 14,400 bytes of public framing per 100 MiB is accepted for faster cancellation and smaller aligned-edit amplification.",
